A complete multi-layer diagnostic sweep of the `ospf_enterprise_static` lab found no fault in any component. Phase 1 ICMP reachability showed 0% loss across all sampled host-to-host and host-to-server paths. Phase A infrastructure triage (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed clean L2 identity, no ACL drop rules, no static ARP poisoning, no addressing or resolver misconfiguration, and all hosts properly assigned IPs with working default routes. Phase B control-plane triage (ospf_snapshot) confirmed all six FRR routers have healthy daemons with full OSPF adjacencies and a consistent nine-route table each — no missing neighbors, no area mismatches, no FRR service issues. Phase D service triage (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) confirmed that all four web servers (web0.local–web3.local) respond HTTP 200 in under 5 ms from both `host_1_1_1_1` and `host_2_1_1_1`, DNS resolves all hostnames correctly to their expected IPs via the shared resolver at 10.200.0.2, and no device shows CPU pressure, stress-tool injection, or elevated socket counts. There is no tc shaping rule, no iptables/nftables filter, no carrier flap mechanism, and no crashed process anywhere in the topology. The helpdesk report ("the internal page is dead again") describes a symptom that is not reproducible in the current lab state and leaves no mechanism or trace behind. Root cause: no active fault — the system is fully operational at time of inspection. Fix: no corrective action is required; the user should retry access and, if the problem recurs, capture the specific error message or HTTP status code so the intermittent condition can be correlated against live diagnostics.
